-- A+ Core 2 (220-1102) Domain 3 (Software Troubleshooting) expansion.
-- Adds new questions a2-q-900..959 (60) and flashcards a2-f-900..972 (73)
-- across all five objectives: 3.1 Windows OS problems, 3.2 PC security
-- issues, 3.3 malware-removal best practices, 3.4 mobile OS/app issues,
-- 3.5 mobile OS security issues. Existing content is untouched.

insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
-- ==== 3.1 Windows OS problems (9) ====
('a2-q-900','aplus2',3,'3.1','mcq',1,$q$A Windows 10 PC displays a BSOD referencing "PAGE_FAULT_IN_NONPAGED_AREA" shortly after a new RAM stick was installed. What is the MOST likely cause?$q$,$q$["Faulty or incompatible RAM","An expired antivirus subscription","A loose HDMI cable","Insufficient printer ink"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$PAGE_FAULT errors appearing right after a hardware change point at faulty or incompatible memory; running a memory diagnostic and reseating or replacing the RAM addresses the likely cause.$q$),
('a2-q-901','aplus2',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$A desktop shows "Boot Device Not Found" after a storage upgrade. What is the MOST appropriate first step?$q$,$q$["Reinstall Windows from scratch","Enter BIOS/UEFI and verify the drive is detected and the boot order/mode matches the drive's partition style","Replace the power supply","Run Disk Cleanup"]$q$::jsonb,$q$1$q$::jsonb,$q$This error usually means firmware isn't detecting the drive or the boot mode (UEFI/GPT vs Legacy/MBR) mismatches how the drive was partitioned - check BIOS/UEFI detection and boot settings before anything more drastic.$q$),
('a2-q-902','aplus2',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$Task Manager shows disk usage pegged at 100% while CPU and RAM are largely idle, and the PC feels sluggish. What is the BEST first action?$q$,$q$["Sort processes by disk usage in Task Manager or Resource Monitor to find the offending process","Reinstall the operating system","Add more RAM","Replace the motherboard"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$100% disk usage with low CPU/RAM strongly suggests one process (indexing, an update, a scan, or a failing drive) is saturating I/O - identify it in Resource Monitor before escalating to a hardware change.$q$),
('a2-q-903','aplus2',3,'3.1','mcq',1,$q$A background service is configured to start Automatic but shows "Stopped" in services.msc, and the dependent feature isn't working. What is the direct fix attempt?$q$,$q$["Start the service manually from services.msc and check its Dependencies tab","Uninstall Windows entirely","Replace the hard drive","Update the BIOS/UEFI firmware"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$services.msc lets you start a stopped service directly and inspect its dependencies - a failed or disabled dependency is a common reason an Automatic service won't stay running.$q$),
('a2-q-904','aplus2',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$After a cumulative Windows update, a PC no longer boots into the desktop. What is an appropriate, non-destructive remediation?$q$,$q$["Boot into Advanced Startup Options and uninstall the recent update, or use System Restore to a pre-update point","Immediately reinstall Windows from scratch","Replace the CMOS battery","Format the recovery partition"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$A broken update is best undone via Advanced Startup's "Uninstall Updates" option or a pre-update restore point - a full reinstall is disproportionate when a rollback resolves the problem.$q$),
('a2-q-905','aplus2',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$A laptop shuts down unexpectedly whenever the user runs a graphics-intensive game, but is stable for office work. What is the MOST likely cause?$q$,$q$["Overheating causing a thermal shutdown","A corrupted user profile","An expired Windows license","A misconfigured screen saver"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Shutdowns tied specifically to heavy GPU/CPU load point at a thermal protection cutoff - check vents, fans, and thermal paste, and monitor temperatures under load.$q$),
('a2-q-906','aplus2',3,'3.1','multi',2,$q$Which TWO symptoms together most strongly suggest a failing power supply as the cause of a desktop's random shutdowns?$q$,$q$["Shutdowns occur under load, such as gaming or multiple drives spinning up, with no consistent error message","The system also randomly reboots or intermittently fails to power on, even at idle","The desktop wallpaper reverts to default after every restart","File Explorer takes longer to open a folder containing many files"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Random shutdowns or reboots specifically under load, combined with intermittent power-on failures at idle, both point to inconsistent power delivery; wallpaper resets and slow folder browsing are unrelated cosmetic or performance symptoms.$q$),
('a2-q-907','aplus2',3,'3.1','ordering',3,$q$Order the general troubleshooting steps for a Windows machine producing unpredictable BSODs, from first to last.$q$,$q$["Note the stop code and check Reliability Monitor or Event Viewer for the failing driver or module","Boot into Safe Mode to see whether the crashes stop","Roll back or update the most recently changed driver identified as the likely cause","Run hardware diagnostics, such as a memory test or disk check, if crashes persist after driver remediation"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Start by identifying what actually crashed via the stop code and faulting module, confirm whether the minimal-driver Safe Mode environment is stable, fix the implicated driver, and only escalate to memory or disk diagnostics if crashes continue.$q$),
('a2-q-908','aplus2',3,'3.1','matching',2,$q$Match each Windows OS symptom to its MOST likely underlying cause.$q$,$q${"left":["A BSOD appears right after a new video driver is installed","A PC will not boot to an installed OS after a failed storage migration","A system randomly powers off only during CPU-intensive tasks","A critical Windows service shows Stopped and will not stay running"],"right":["The new driver is incompatible or corrupted","Boot files or the partition table were not correctly configured for the new drive","Thermal shutdown from inadequate cooling","A required dependency service is disabled or failing"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Symptom timing is diagnostic: a crash right after a driver change implicates that driver, a boot failure after a storage swap points to boot configuration, heat-triggered shutdowns implicate cooling, and a service that won't stay running often has a broken dependency.$q$),
-- ==== 3.2 PC security issues (12) ====
('a2-q-909','aplus2',3,'3.2','mcq',1,$q$A user reports that every time they type a web address, they land on an unfamiliar search engine instead. What does this MOST likely indicate?$q$,$q$["Browser hijacking/redirection, often from a malicious extension or altered proxy settings","A normal ISP outage","The user mistyped the address each time","A Windows Update currently in progress"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Search or URL redirection to an unexpected site is a classic browser-hijack symptom, usually caused by a malicious extension, add-on, or altered proxy/DNS settings - not a typo or a temporary outage.$q$),
('a2-q-910','aplus2',3,'3.2','mcq',1,$q$A user sees frequent pop-up ads even when no browser window is open. What does this MOST likely indicate?$q$,$q$["Adware installed on the system generating ads outside the browser","A failing monitor","Normal Windows notification behavior","A printer driver conflict"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Pop-ups appearing outside of any browser window point to adware running as a background process on the system itself, rather than a browser-only issue.$q$),
('a2-q-911','aplus2',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$A user calls saying a full-screen alert appeared claiming their computer has several viruses and includes a countdown timer urging them to call a phone number now. What is this?$q$,$q$["A rogue antivirus, or scareware, attempting to scare the user into calling or paying","A legitimate Windows Defender notification","A hardware failure warning generated by the BIOS/UEFI","A scheduled disk-check prompt"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Urgent, alarming "viruses found - call now" messages with countdown timers are the hallmark of scareware/rogue antivirus - genuine security software never demands a phone call or payment.$q$),
('a2-q-912','aplus2',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$A PC that used to boot in under a minute now takes several minutes, fans run constantly, and Task Manager shows an unfamiliar process consuming high CPU even while the user is idle. What does this MOST likely indicate?$q$,$q$["Malware running in the background and consuming system resources","A Windows Update that finished successfully","A monitor resolution mismatch","Normal aging of a hard drive"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Sudden, unexplained slow performance paired with an unrecognized high-CPU process at idle is a strong sign of malware, such as a cryptominer or bot, consuming resources in the background.$q$),
('a2-q-913','aplus2',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$A user reports that their contacts are receiving spam email "from" their account that the user never sent. What does this indicate?$q$,$q$["The email account has likely been hijacked or compromised","The user's contacts have a virus, not the user","This is normal email server behavior","The user's antivirus definitions are only slightly outdated"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Outgoing spam the account owner never sent is the signature symptom of a hijacked email account - the credentials or an authorized app/session token were likely compromised and should be secured immediately.$q$),
('a2-q-914','aplus2',3,'3.2','mcq',3,$q$A user's browser homepage keeps resetting to an unwanted page every time it is changed back, and a new toolbar appeared that the user never installed. What is the BEST explanation?$q$,$q$["A malicious browser extension or hijacker is persistently overriding browser settings","The browser needs to be reinstalled for an unrelated reason","This is a display driver bug","The user's ISP silently changed their DNS servers"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Settings that keep reverting, combined with an unrecognized toolbar, point squarely at a browser hijacker actively re-applying its configuration - not a display or ISP-level issue.$q$),
('a2-q-915','aplus2',3,'3.2','mcq',1,$q$Which of the following is the CLEAREST sign of a rogue/fake antivirus program rather than a legitimate installed AV product?$q$,$q$["It demands immediate payment or a phone call to remove threats it claims to have found","It runs a scheduled scan overnight","It shows a confirmation after a completed clean scan","It updates its virus definitions automatically"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Legitimate antivirus products don't pressure users into instant payment or a phone call; that high-pressure tactic is the giveaway of scareware or rogue security software.$q$),
('a2-q-916','aplus2',3,'3.2','multi',2,$q$A user's PC shows browser redirects to unfamiliar search pages and an unrecognized toolbar in the browser. Which TWO actions are appropriate FIRST checks?$q$,$q$["Review installed browser extensions/add-ons for anything unfamiliar","Check the browser's proxy and DNS settings for unauthorized changes","Replace the monitor","Reinstall the printer driver"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Redirects and rogue toolbars are typically caused by malicious extensions or altered proxy/DNS settings, so both should be inspected directly - display and printer hardware are unrelated to this symptom.$q$),
('a2-q-917','aplus2',3,'3.2','multi',2,$q$Which TWO of the following are classic signs a system has been infected with malware causing unexpected slow performance?$q$,$q$["Fans run at high speed and CPU usage stays elevated even when no applications are open","Disk activity light stays constantly lit with no user-initiated file operations underway","The user recently changed their desktop wallpaper","A new printer was added to the list of devices"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Persistent high CPU/fan activity and constant unexplained disk activity at idle both indicate something is running in the background without the user's knowledge - wallpaper changes and printer additions are unrelated, benign events.$q$),
('a2-q-918','aplus2',3,'3.2','mcq',3,$q$A user reports pop-ups claiming a piece of software they don't recall installing has an expired license, along with a noticeably sluggish browser. Which description BEST fits this combination?$q$,$q$["A likely potentially unwanted program (PUP) or adware bundle generating fake alerts and consuming browser resources","Definitely a failing RAM module","A normal software trial expiration notice requiring no action","A sign that the Windows license itself has expired"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Unfamiliar software with fake license/expiration pop-ups is a textbook potentially unwanted program or adware bundle, often silently installed alongside legitimate freeware; it is not a Windows license or RAM issue.$q$),
('a2-q-919','aplus2',3,'3.2','mcq',1,$q$What is the safest immediate action for a user who receives a phone call from someone claiming to be tech support after seeing a fake virus alert pop-up?$q$,$q$["Hang up and avoid providing remote access, payment, or personal information","Grant them remote access so they can \"fix\" the issue","Read them the computer's administrator password","Pay the requested fee to close the alert"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Scareware pop-ups and the associated cold-call scams are social engineering - the correct response is to disengage entirely and never grant remote access, credentials, or payment based on an unsolicited alert.$q$),
('a2-q-920','aplus2',3,'3.2','matching',2,$q$Match each PC security symptom a user reports to its MOST likely underlying issue.$q$,$q${"left":["The browser opens to a different search engine than the one set as default","A full-screen warning demands payment to remove detected viruses","Contacts report receiving spam that appears to come from the user's own email address","The system is far slower than usual, with an unfamiliar process pinning the CPU"],"right":["A browser hijack via a malicious extension or altered settings","A rogue antivirus, or scareware","A hijacked email account","Background malware consuming resources"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Each symptom maps to a distinct PC security issue: redirected search behavior is a hijack, payment-demanding alerts are scareware, spam sent from the user's own account is a hijacked mailbox, and unexplained resource drain from an unknown process is background malware activity.$q$),
-- ==== 3.3 Malware-removal best practices (11) ====
('a2-q-921','aplus2',3,'3.3','ordering',2,$q$A technician confirms a workstation is infected. Place the seven remediation steps in the correct order, from first action to last.$q$,$q$["Investigate and verify malware symptoms","Quarantine the infected system","Disable System Restore (in Windows)","Remediate the infected system: update anti-malware software and scan/remove","Schedule scans and run updates","Re-enable System Restore and create a new restore point","Educate the end user"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]$q$::jsonb,$q$The official order is investigate/verify, quarantine, disable System Restore, remediate, schedule scans/updates, re-enable System Restore, then educate the user - each step depends on the one before it, since remediating before quarantining or re-enabling System Restore before remediation both undermine the cleanup.$q$),
('a2-q-922','aplus2',3,'3.3','mcq',1,$q$In the malware-removal process, what should a technician do immediately after confirming symptoms indicate malware, and BEFORE attempting any remediation?$q$,$q$["Quarantine the infected system by disconnecting it from the network","Immediately run a full antivirus scan while still connected to the network","Reformat the drive right away","Contact the end user's manager"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Quarantine comes right after identification and verification - isolating the system, typically by disconnecting network access, prevents the malware from spreading to other machines before any cleanup begins.$q$),
('a2-q-923','aplus2',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$Why does the malware-removal process call for disabling System Restore BEFORE remediation, rather than after?$q$,$q$["Existing restore points may already contain the malware, so remediating first without disabling it risks the infection surviving in a saved restore point","System Restore uses too much CPU during a scan","System Restore must always be off by default on Windows","Antivirus software cannot run while System Restore is enabled"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$If System Restore stays enabled during remediation, an existing infected restore point could later be used to roll the system back into a reinfected state - disabling it first protects the cleanup from being undone.$q$),
('a2-q-924','aplus2',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$After remediation is complete and the system tests clean, what is the NEXT step in the process, before re-enabling System Restore?$q$,$q$["Schedule scans and run updates","Immediately educate the end user and close the ticket","Format the drive","Disable the user's account"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$The process schedules ongoing scans and applies updates before re-enabling System Restore and creating a fresh restore point, ensuring ongoing protection is in place before that new clean baseline is set.$q$),
('a2-q-925','aplus2',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$Why is it important to create a NEW restore point immediately after re-enabling System Restore during malware remediation?$q$,$q$["So a future rollback restores to a known-clean state rather than to an old or potentially infected point","Windows requires a restore point in order to boot","It speeds up the next Windows Update","It re-activates the Windows license"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Creating a fresh restore point right after re-enabling the feature establishes a clean baseline - a future rollback then lands on a known-good, malware-free state rather than an old point.$q$),
('a2-q-926','aplus2',3,'3.3','mcq',3,$q$A technician cleans an infected PC but skips the final "educate the user" step. What risk does this create?$q$,$q$["The user, unaware of what led to the infection, is likely to repeat the same behavior and become reinfected","The antivirus software will stop functioning","The system will fail to boot","Windows Update will be permanently disabled"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Educating the user on how the infection likely occurred and how to avoid it is what prevents a recurrence - skipping it leaves the root behavioral cause unaddressed even though the technical infection is gone.$q$),
('a2-q-927','aplus2',3,'3.3','multi',2,$q$Which TWO of the following are true about the quarantine step of the malware-removal process?$q$,$q$["It typically involves disconnecting the infected system from the network to prevent lateral spread","It should happen before remediation, not after","It means immediately deleting all of the user's files on the system","It replaces the need for any antivirus scan afterward"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Quarantine means isolating the machine, usually by disconnecting it from the network, and happens early, before remediation - it does not involve deleting user data, and a scan/removal step is still required afterward.$q$),
('a2-q-928','aplus2',3,'3.3','mcq',1,$q$During malware remediation, what does "remediate the infected system" specifically involve?$q$,$q$["Updating anti-malware software's definitions and running a scan to identify and remove the malicious files or processes","Reinstalling the operating system from scratch every time","Changing the user's desktop wallpaper","Renaming the infected files without deleting them"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Remediation means updating the anti-malware tool's definitions so it recognizes current threats, then scanning and removing or quarantining the malicious files or processes found.$q$),
('a2-q-929','aplus2',3,'3.3','ordering',3,$q$A technician has just quarantined an infected laptop. Order the remaining steps of the removal process from this point forward.$q$,$q$["Disable System Restore","Remediate: update anti-malware definitions and scan/remove the infection","Schedule future scans and run OS/software updates","Re-enable System Restore and create a new restore point","Educate the end user on how to avoid reinfection"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$After quarantine, the remaining sequence is: disable System Restore to protect against a reinfecting rollback, remediate, schedule ongoing scans/updates, re-enable System Restore with a fresh point, and finally educate the user.$q$),
('a2-q-930','aplus2',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$Before moving to the "schedule scans/updates" step, what should a technician confirm about the remediation just performed?$q$,$q$["That a follow-up scan comes back clean and the symptoms that indicated infection are gone","That the user's password has been changed","That the case has been physically opened and cleaned of dust","That the monitor brightness has been reset"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Before moving on, confirm the remediation actually worked with a clean follow-up scan and the absence of the original symptoms - otherwise scheduling future protection on a still-infected system doesn't address the current problem.$q$),
('a2-q-931','aplus2',3,'3.3','matching',2,$q$Match each step of the malware-removal process to its purpose.$q$,$q${"left":["Quarantine the infected system","Disable System Restore","Schedule scans and run updates","Educate the end user"],"right":["Prevent the infection from spreading to other systems on the network","Prevent an infected state from being preserved for a later reinfecting rollback","Maintain ongoing protection after the immediate cleanup","Reduce the chance the same infection vector is used again"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Each step serves a distinct purpose: quarantine contains spread, disabling System Restore protects the cleanup from being undone, scheduling scans/updates sustains protection going forward, and education addresses the human factor behind the infection.$q$),
-- ==== 3.4 Mobile OS/app issues (13) ====
('a2-q-932','aplus2',3,'3.4','mcq',1,$q$A mobile app crashes immediately every time it's opened, right after the phone's OS was updated. What is the BEST first troubleshooting step?$q$,$q$["Check for an app update or known compatibility fix from the developer","Factory reset the phone","Replace the battery","Disable the phone's Wi-Fi"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Crashes appearing right after an OS update usually mean the app hasn't caught up with an OS change; checking for a compatibility update from the developer is the direct, low-risk first step.$q$),
('a2-q-933','aplus2',3,'3.4','mcq',1,$q$A user's phone battery, which used to last all day, now drains within a few hours with no change in usage habits. What should be checked FIRST?$q$,$q$["Battery usage statistics to identify which app or process is consuming excess power","Replace the phone immediately","Reinstall the operating system","Turn off the phone's screen lock"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Battery usage statistics reveal the specific app or service driving the drain, whether a misbehaving app, malware, or a stuck background process, so it can be targeted directly rather than guessing.$q$),
('a2-q-934','aplus2',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$A smartphone repeatedly drops its Wi-Fi connection in a location where other devices connect fine. What is the MOST likely cause to check first?$q$,$q$["The phone's saved Wi-Fi network profile/settings for that network","The user's cellular data plan","The phone's screen brightness setting","The phone's app store login"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$If other devices connect fine in the same location, the fault most likely lies with that phone's specific saved network profile - forgetting and re-adding the network is a common fix - rather than the router or an unrelated setting.$q$),
('a2-q-935','aplus2',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$A user notices an app has both camera and microphone permission enabled, though they don't recall granting it. What does this indicate, and what is the appropriate response?$q$,$q$["A potential unauthorized access/privacy risk - review and revoke unnecessary app permissions","This is normal default behavior for every app and needs no action","The phone's warranty has expired","The app needs an update to fix a display bug"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Unexpected sensitive permissions on an app the user doesn't trust or recall approving is a privacy/security concern; reviewing and revoking unnecessary permissions in the device's settings is the right response.$q$),
('a2-q-936','aplus2',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$A user's mobile data usage report shows several gigabytes consumed by an app that should only sync small amounts of text data. What does this MOST likely suggest?$q$,$q$["The app may be malicious or misconfigured, using data for something other than its intended purpose","The carrier miscounted the bill","The phone's screen resolution is set too high","The SIM card needs replacing"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$A large mismatch between an app's expected data use and its actual consumption is a red flag for malware, a misconfigured sync setting, or excessive background activity, and the app should be investigated or removed.$q$),
('a2-q-937','aplus2',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$A phone runs noticeably slower than usual, with apps taking longer to open and occasional freezes, despite no new apps being installed. What is a reasonable FIRST troubleshooting step?$q$,$q$["Check for background apps consuming excess resources and check available storage space","Immediately perform a factory reset","Replace the phone's screen","Disable the lock screen PIN"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Background app buildup and low free storage are common, easily checked causes of gradual mobile slowdown; a factory reset is a valid escalation but not the first, least-disruptive step.$q$),
('a2-q-938','aplus2',3,'3.4','mcq',3,$q$An Android app repeatedly force-closes with an "Unfortunately, [App] has stopped" message, and clearing the app's cache doesn't help. What is the next reasonable step before uninstalling?$q$,$q$["Clear the app's data, which resets it further than cache, or reinstall the app","Replace the phone's battery","Format the phone's internal storage entirely","Downgrade the phone's OS to an older version"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$If clearing cache alone doesn't resolve repeated crashes, clearing the app's stored data (a deeper reset) or a clean reinstall is the next reasonable step, well short of a full storage wipe or OS downgrade.$q$),
('a2-q-939','aplus2',3,'3.4','mcq',1,$q$A user reports their phone's battery feels unusually hot even when the phone isn't actively being used. What does this MOST likely suggest?$q$,$q$["A misbehaving app or process is actively running/consuming CPU in the background","This is completely normal phone behavior at all times","The phone needs a new SIM card","The screen protector is causing the heat"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Unexplained heat while supposedly idle usually means something, such as a stuck app, malware, or excessive background sync, is actively using the CPU - check battery/CPU usage stats to identify it.$q$),
('a2-q-940','aplus2',3,'3.4','multi',2,$q$Which TWO symptoms together suggest unauthorized access to a user's mobile device or accounts, rather than a simple performance issue?$q$,$q$["The user notices login notifications or account activity from a location/device they don't recognize","Apps the user didn't install appear on the device","The battery lasts a little less long after a recent OS update","The screen brightness auto-adjusts differently than before"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Unfamiliar login activity and apps appearing that the user never installed are both strong indicators of unauthorized access; a slightly shorter battery life after an OS update or a brightness behavior change are unrelated, benign changes.$q$),
('a2-q-941','aplus2',3,'3.4','multi',2,$q$Which TWO of the following are plausible causes of unexpectedly high mobile data usage?$q$,$q$["An app configured to sync large photo or video files over cellular instead of waiting for Wi-Fi","Malware performing background network activity without the user's knowledge","The user changed their phone's wallpaper","The phone's clock is set to the wrong time zone"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Both a misconfigured sync-over-cellular setting and malicious background network activity directly consume data; changing wallpaper or an incorrect time zone have no meaningful effect on data usage.$q$),
('a2-q-942','aplus2',3,'3.4','mcq',3,$q$A user's phone shows normal performance on Wi-Fi but is extremely slow and drops connections whenever on cellular data in an area with full signal bars. What should be checked?$q$,$q$["APN (Access Point Name) settings and whether a carrier settings update is available","The phone's screen timeout setting","The user's app store password","The phone's Bluetooth pairing list"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Full signal but poor cellular data performance points to a data-path configuration issue, such as incorrect or corrupted APN settings or a missing carrier settings update, rather than an unrelated Bluetooth or screen setting.$q$),
('a2-q-943','aplus2',3,'3.4','ordering',2,$q$Order the reasonable troubleshooting escalation for a mobile app that keeps crashing, from least to most disruptive.$q$,$q$["Restart the app, or restart the phone","Clear the app's cache","Clear the app's data or reinstall the app","Factory reset the entire device"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Escalation should move from the least disruptive fix, a simple restart, through clearing cache, then app data/reinstall, and only to a full factory reset if nothing less drastic resolves the issue.$q$),
('a2-q-944','aplus2',3,'3.4','matching',2,$q$Match each mobile symptom to its most likely category of issue.$q$,$q${"left":["An app force-closes every time it's opened after an OS update","Battery life has dropped sharply with no change in usage","Data usage for one app is far higher than expected for its function","The device shows login activity from an unrecognized location"],"right":["An app compatibility issue","Battery drain from a misbehaving app or process","Unexpected or excessive data usage","Unauthorized account access"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Each symptom category has a distinct signature: post-update crashes indicate compatibility problems, a sharp unexplained battery drop points to a resource-hungry app, disproportionate data use flags misuse of the network, and unfamiliar login activity flags unauthorized access.$q$),
-- ==== 3.5 Mobile OS security issues (15) ====
('a2-q-945','aplus2',3,'3.5','mcq',1,$q$A user notices a location icon persistently active in their phone's status bar even when they're not using navigation or maps. What does this suggest?$q$,$q$["An app is tracking the device's location in the background, possibly without the user's informed consent","The GPS chip is defective and needs replacement","This is normal, unavoidable phone behavior","The phone's clock is out of sync"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$A persistent location indicator with no navigation app in active use suggests an app is accessing location in the background - the user should review app location permissions and revoke unnecessary background access.$q$),
('a2-q-946','aplus2',3,'3.5','mcq',2,$q$A phone shows unusually high background network traffic even when the user isn't actively using any apps. What is this MOST likely a sign of?$q$,$q$["Spyware or malware exfiltrating data or communicating with a remote server","A software update that finished successfully with no lingering effect","The battery is simply fully charged","The phone's screen brightness is set to automatic"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Sustained, unexplained network activity while the device is otherwise idle is a classic indicator of spyware or malware sending data out or communicating with a command-and-control server.$q$),
('a2-q-947','aplus2',3,'3.5','mcq',2,$q$A user discovers that personal photos from their phone have appeared online without their consent. What security issue does this indicate?$q$,$q$["Leaked personal files, likely from a compromised cloud backup account, a malicious app, or unauthorized device access","A normal result of using cloud photo backup","The phone's screen resolution setting","An expected outcome of a factory reset"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Personal files appearing publicly without consent points to a leak - commonly through a compromised cloud account tied to the device, an app with excessive storage access, or unauthorized physical/remote access to the device.$q$),
('a2-q-948','aplus2',3,'3.5','mcq',2,$q$A user gets an alert that their cloud account, linked to their phone, was signed into from an unfamiliar device in another country. What does this indicate?$q$,$q$["Unauthorized account access - the account credentials are likely compromised","A normal notification sent to all users periodically","The phone's time zone setting is incorrect","The phone needs a software update"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Sign-in alerts from unrecognized devices or locations are a direct sign the account credentials have been compromised; the user should change the password and enable multi-factor authentication immediately.$q$),
('a2-q-949','aplus2',3,'3.5','mcq',2,$q$A technician finds superuser/root access enabled on a user's Android phone, and the user says they never did this intentionally. What is the security concern?$q$,$q$["Unauthorized rooting can bypass built-in OS security controls and expose the device to greater risk","Rooting always improves phone security","Root access is required for all app installations","This has no security implications"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Root (or jailbreak on iOS) access removes OS-level security sandboxing; if it happened without the user's intent, malware may have escalated its own privileges, which significantly increases risk and warrants investigation.$q$),
('a2-q-950','aplus2',3,'3.5','mcq',3,$q$An iPhone is discovered to be jailbroken without the owner's knowledge, and several apps outside the official App Store are installed. What is the MOST significant security risk this creates?$q$,$q$["Apps installed outside official vetting and sandboxing can access data and system functions normally restricted, increasing exposure to malware","The phone's battery will drain slower","iOS updates will install faster","The phone's warranty is automatically extended"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Jailbreaking removes Apple's sandboxing and app-vetting protections, allowing unofficial apps broader system access - this is precisely why an unauthorized jailbreak is treated as a serious security compromise.$q$),
('a2-q-951','aplus2',3,'3.5','mcq',1,$q$What is the FIRST recommended action when a user's cloud account tied to their phone shows signs of unauthorized access?$q$,$q$["Change the account password immediately and enable multi-factor authentication","Wait a few days to see if it happens again","Turn off the phone's Wi-Fi permanently","Uninstall all apps from the phone"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Immediately changing the password and enabling MFA directly cuts off the attacker's access path; waiting or unrelated actions don't address the compromised credential.$q$),
('a2-q-952','aplus2',3,'3.5','multi',2,$q$Which TWO of the following are appropriate responses when unauthorized root/jailbreak access is discovered on a managed corporate device?$q$,$q$["Isolate or remove the device from corporate network and resources until it can be re-imaged or restored to a known-good state","Report the finding per organizational security policy and incident procedures","Allow the device to remain connected since rooting is a user preference","Ignore it since root access doesn't affect app permissions"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$An unauthorized root/jailbreak on a managed device should be treated as a security incident - isolate the device and follow reporting/incident-response procedures; allowing continued access or dismissing the risk are both incorrect responses.$q$),
('a2-q-953','aplus2',3,'3.5','multi',2,$q$Which TWO symptoms together most strongly suggest a mobile device has spyware performing unauthorized location tracking and data exfiltration?$q$,$q$["The location indicator activates even when no mapping or ride-share app is in active use","Background data usage is far higher than the user's typical pattern with no obvious cause","The user recently updated a single trusted app from the official store","The phone's ringtone volume reset to default after a restart"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$An active location indicator without a legitimate app in use, combined with unexplained high background data usage, together point at an app quietly collecting and sending data - the other two options are unrelated, benign occurrences.$q$),
('a2-q-954','aplus2',3,'3.5','mcq',2,$q$A user's phone contacts list and private messages appear to have been accessed by an app that has no legitimate reason to need that data. What should be checked?$q$,$q$["The app's requested/granted permissions, such as contacts, SMS, and storage, against what it actually needs to function","The phone's screen timeout setting","The Wi-Fi router's SSID broadcast setting","The phone's date and time settings"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Excessive permissions relative to an app's actual function are the mechanism by which such apps access data they shouldn't - reviewing and revoking unnecessary permissions directly addresses the risk.$q$),
('a2-q-955','aplus2',3,'3.5','mcq',1,$q$What is the MOST direct way to check whether an app is unnecessarily tracking a phone's location in the background?$q$,$q$["Review the device's app permission settings for location access, specifically checking for \"always allow\" background access","Restart the phone","Update all installed apps","Change the phone's wallpaper"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$The OS's app permissions settings show exactly which apps have location access and whether it's limited to while-in-use or granted as always-on background access, the latter being the setting most worth scrutinizing.$q$),
('a2-q-956','aplus2',3,'3.5','mcq',3,$q$A corporate-owned Android device is found to be rooted, and the MDM agent reports it can no longer enforce policy on the device. What does this indicate about the impact of unauthorized rooting?$q$,$q$["Rooting can undermine or bypass MDM security controls and compliance enforcement, a major reason organizations treat it as a critical security event","MDM software always continues to function normally regardless of root status","Rooting only affects battery life, not policy enforcement","This means the MDM software itself is broken and unrelated to root status"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Root access can disable or circumvent the very security agent meant to enforce corporate policy, which is exactly why organizations treat unauthorized rooting on managed devices as a serious, immediate security concern.$q$),
('a2-q-957','aplus2',3,'3.5','ordering',2,$q$Order the appropriate response steps when a user reports their phone's cloud-linked account was accessed from an unrecognized device.$q$,$q$["Verify the alert is legitimate and not itself a phishing attempt","Change the account password immediately","Enable or verify multi-factor authentication is active","Review recent account activity and connected devices/sessions for other signs of compromise","Sign out of all other active sessions/devices"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$First confirm the alert itself isn't a phishing lure, then cut off access by changing the password, harden the account with MFA, review activity for the scope of compromise, and sign out other sessions to remove lingering unauthorized access.$q$),
('a2-q-958','aplus2',3,'3.5','matching',2,$q$Match each mobile security symptom to its most likely underlying issue.$q$,$q${"left":["A location indicator stays active with no mapping app in use","Background network usage is unexpectedly and consistently high","Personal photos appear online without the user's consent","The device shows escalated/root privileges the user never enabled"],"right":["Unauthorized location tracking by an app","Spyware or malware exfiltrating data","Leaked personal files, often via a compromised account or app","Unauthorized rooting, likely by malware or an unknown action"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Each symptom maps to a specific mobile security issue: a persistent location indicator without an active mapping app signals background tracking, sustained unexplained network use signals exfiltration, leaked photos point to a compromised account or overly permissioned app, and unexplained root access points to a privilege-escalation compromise.$q$),
('a2-q-959','aplus2',3,'3.5','mcq',2,$q$Why is unauthorized root/jailbreak access considered a more severe issue than a single misbehaving app?$q$,$q$["It removes OS-level sandboxing and security boundaries system-wide, potentially exposing every app and all data on the device rather than just one app's scope","It only affects the specific app that triggered it","It automatically fixes itself after a reboot","It has no effect on other installed apps"]$q$::jsonb,$q$0$q$::jsonb,$q$Root/jailbreak access breaks the OS's fundamental security model across the entire device, not just for one app - this systemic exposure is why it's treated as a higher-severity compromise than an isolated misbehaving app.$q$);

insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
-- ==== 3.1 Windows OS problems (11) ====
('a2-f-900','aplus2','core',3,'3.1',$$A PC shows a blue screen referencing a stop code immediately after a driver update. What's the fastest way to confirm the driver caused it?$$,$$Boot into Safe Mode, which loads only core drivers, or check Reliability Monitor/Event Viewer for the driver named at the crash; if the machine is stable in Safe Mode or the log names that driver, roll it back or update it.$$),
('a2-f-901','aplus2','core',3,'3.1',$$What does "Boot Device Not Found" typically indicate, and what should you check first?$$,$$The firmware cannot find a bootable drive - check that the drive is detected in BIOS/UEFI, the boot order lists it, and the boot mode (UEFI/GPT vs Legacy/MBR) matches how the drive was partitioned.$$),
('a2-f-902','aplus2','core',3,'3.1',$$Task Manager shows 100% disk usage with low CPU/RAM and the system feels sluggish. What's the diagnostic approach?$$,$$Open Resource Monitor or sort Task Manager's Processes tab by Disk to find which process is generating the I/O - common culprits are Windows Search indexing, Windows Update, antivirus scans, or a failing drive - rather than guessing at a hardware upgrade.$$),
('a2-f-903','aplus2','core',3,'3.1',$$A Windows service set to Automatic keeps stopping. Where do you look, and what's a common root cause?$$,$$services.msc shows the current state and lets you start it manually; check the Dependencies tab - a disabled or failed dependent service is a frequent reason an Automatic service won't stay running.$$),
('a2-f-904','aplus2','core',3,'3.1',$$A cumulative Windows update leaves a PC unable to reach the desktop. What are two non-destructive fixes to try before reinstalling?$$,$$From Advanced Startup Options/Windows RE, either uninstall the recent update or apply System Restore to a point before the update - both are far less disruptive than a full OS reinstall.$$),
('a2-f-905','aplus2','core',3,'3.1',$$A laptop shuts down only during graphics-intensive tasks but runs fine otherwise. What's the likely cause and check?$$,$$Load-triggered shutdowns point to a thermal protection cutoff; check that vents and fans aren't clogged, verify thermal paste condition, and monitor temperatures under load with a diagnostic utility.$$),
('a2-f-906','aplus2','core',3,'3.1',$$What combination of symptoms points to a failing power supply rather than a software issue?$$,$$Random shutdowns or reboots specifically under heavier load, plus intermittent failure to power on even at idle, both point at inconsistent power delivery rather than a driver, update, or software fault.$$),
('a2-f-907','aplus2','core',3,'3.1',$$Where can you find which driver or module was responsible for a recent Windows crash?$$,$$Reliability Monitor gives a timeline of crashes and their source; Event Viewer's System log records the stop code and often the faulting driver name for deeper investigation.$$),
('a2-f-908','aplus2','acronym',3,'3.1',$$BSOD$$,$$Blue Screen of Death - a Windows stop error, a kernel-level fault that halts the system and displays a stop code identifying the failure, used to diagnose driver, hardware, or system-file problems.$$),
('a2-f-909','aplus2','acronym',3,'3.1',$$POST$$,$$Power-On Self Test - the firmware-level hardware check a PC runs before handing control to the boot loader; a failure here, shown as beeps or codes, means the fault is with hardware, not yet the OS.$$),
('a2-f-910','aplus2','feynman',3,'3.1',$$Explain why a spontaneous shutdown that only happens under heavy load points to a different root cause than one that happens randomly at idle.$$,$$Model answer: A load-triggered shutdown correlates with increased power draw and heat generation - components run hotter and the power supply is stressed harder, so thermal protection or marginal power delivery is implicated. A shutdown that happens at idle, with no load pattern, is less likely to be heat-related and points instead toward intermittent hardware faults, such as loose connections or a failing motherboard component, regardless of load. The timing pattern itself is diagnostic evidence that narrows where to look before any parts get replaced.$$),
-- ==== 3.2 PC security issues (14) ====
('a2-f-911','aplus2','core',3,'3.2',$$A user's browser keeps redirecting searches to an unfamiliar site. What does this symptom indicate and where should you look?$$,$$This is browser redirection/hijacking - check installed extensions/add-ons for anything unrecognized, and review the browser's proxy and default search engine settings for unauthorized changes.$$),
('a2-f-912','aplus2','core',3,'3.2',$$What is a rogue antivirus, or scareware, and how does a user typically encounter it?$$,$$A rogue antivirus is malicious software or a web page that mimics a legitimate security alert, claiming to have found infections and pressuring the user to pay or call a number immediately - the urgency and payment demand are the tell, since real AV never operates that way.$$),
('a2-f-913','aplus2','core',3,'3.2',$$A user says pop-up ads appear even when no browser window is open. What does this indicate?$$,$$Ads appearing outside any browser window point to adware running as a standalone background process on the system, rather than a browser-specific problem, so it needs to be found and removed at the OS level.$$),
('a2-f-914','aplus2','core',3,'3.2',$$What does it mean when a user's contacts start receiving spam email that appears to come from the user, but the user never sent it?$$,$$This indicates a hijacked email account - the account's credentials or an authorized app/session token were compromised and are being used to send spam; the fix starts with changing the password and revoking suspicious app access.$$),
('a2-f-915','aplus2','core',3,'3.2',$$A PC becomes unexpectedly slow with no new software installed by the user, and Task Manager shows a CPU-heavy process the user doesn't recognize. What does this suggest?$$,$$This is a common symptom of malware infection - background processes like cryptominers, bots, or spyware consume CPU, memory, or network resources without the user's knowledge, causing otherwise-unexplained slowdown.$$),
('a2-f-916','aplus2','core',3,'3.2',$$Why is a persistent, unremovable browser toolbar a red flag?$$,$$A toolbar the user didn't intentionally install and can't cleanly remove usually indicates a potentially unwanted program (PUP) or hijacker bundled with other software, often also responsible for redirects or pop-ups.$$),
('a2-f-917','aplus2','core',3,'3.2',$$What should a technician tell a user who receives an unsolicited phone call claiming to be tech support after a scary pop-up appeared?$$,$$Advise the user to hang up immediately and never grant remote access, provide payment, or share credentials based on an unsolicited call or alert - this is a social-engineering scam riding on the scareware pop-up.$$),
('a2-f-918','aplus2','core',3,'3.2',$$A user reports their homepage keeps changing back to an unwanted page every time they reset it. What's happening?$$,$$A malicious browser extension or hijacker is actively re-applying its configuration each time the user changes it back - simply resetting the homepage repeatedly will not fix it until the underlying extension or setting is removed.$$),
('a2-f-919','aplus2','core',3,'3.2',$$How can you distinguish a legitimate Windows security notification from a fake/scareware pop-up?$$,$$Legitimate notifications come through the OS's own notification center or a known, already-installed AV product, never demand immediate payment or a phone call, and don't use full-screen countdown timers designed to induce panic.$$),
('a2-f-920','aplus2','core',3,'3.2',$$A user notices unusually high outbound network activity and their Sent folder contains messages they didn't write. What two PC security issues does this suggest together?$$,$$This suggests a hijacked email account, causing unauthorized sending, potentially combined with malware causing unexpected data usage or traffic - both should be investigated, starting with securing the email account and scanning the endpoint.$$),
('a2-f-921','aplus2','acronym',3,'3.2',$$PUP$$,$$Potentially Unwanted Program - software, often bundled with legitimate installers, that isn't strictly malicious but causes symptoms like pop-ups, toolbars, browser hijacking, or slowdowns, and that users typically didn't intend to install.$$),
('a2-f-922','aplus2','acronym',3,'3.2',$$AV$$,$$AntiVirus - security software that detects and removes malware; "rogue AV" or scareware impersonates a legitimate AV alert to scare users into paying for fake removal services or calling a scam support line.$$),
('a2-f-923','aplus2','feynman',3,'3.2',$$Explain why browser redirection and pop-up ads are usually a symptom of malware rather than a broken browser.$$,$$Model answer: A browser bug produces consistent, predictable glitches unrelated to specific sites or intent. Redirection and injected pop-ups instead follow a pattern that benefits an attacker - traffic gets funneled to specific ad networks or fake pages, which only makes sense if something is deliberately intercepting requests or injecting content. That something is typically a malicious extension, altered proxy/DNS settings, or adware installed, often bundled without clear consent, specifically to monetize the user's browsing - which is why the fix is to find and remove that software rather than reinstalling or repairing the browser itself.$$),
('a2-f-924','aplus2','feynman',3,'3.2',$$Explain why hijacked email is dangerous even if the user's own PC seems otherwise fine.$$,$$Model answer: Email hijacking often happens through credential theft, such as phishing, reused passwords, or a stolen session/OAuth token, rather than malware sitting on the local machine, which is why the PC can appear completely normal. But the attacker now controls a trusted communication channel: they can send convincing phishing to the victim's real contacts, reset passwords on other accounts tied to that email, and read stored messages. The local device being clean doesn't mean the account is safe - securing the account through a password change, revoking app/session access, and enabling MFA has to happen independently of any endpoint scan.$$),
-- ==== 3.3 Malware-removal best practices (14) ====
('a2-f-925','aplus2','core',3,'3.3',$$What is the correct order of the seven-step malware removal process?$$,$$1) Investigate and verify malware symptoms. 2) Quarantine the infected system. 3) Disable System Restore. 4) Remediate: update anti-malware software and scan/remove. 5) Schedule scans and run updates. 6) Re-enable System Restore and create a restore point. 7) Educate the end user.$$),
('a2-f-926','aplus2','core',3,'3.3',$$What does "quarantine the infected system" mean in practice?$$,$$Isolate the machine so the infection cannot spread further - typically by disconnecting it from the network, such as unplugging Ethernet or disabling Wi-Fi, before any remediation begins.$$),
('a2-f-927','aplus2','core',3,'3.3',$$Why must System Restore be disabled before remediation, not after?$$,$$If left enabled during cleanup, existing restore points could contain the malware; disabling it first prevents a later rollback from reintroducing the infection, and also stops new restore points from capturing the still-infected state mid-cleanup.$$),
('a2-f-928','aplus2','core',3,'3.3',$$What two actions make up the "remediate" step of malware removal?$$,$$Update the anti-malware software's definitions so it recognizes current threats, then run a scan to identify and remove, or quarantine, the malicious files and processes found.$$),
('a2-f-929','aplus2','core',3,'3.3',$$Why is scheduling scans and updates a distinct step from remediation itself?$$,$$Remediation removes the current infection; scheduling ongoing scans and keeping the OS/software updated addresses future risk, closing the gaps that allowed the infection in and catching any relapse early.$$),
('a2-f-930','aplus2','core',3,'3.3',$$What should be done immediately after re-enabling System Restore during malware remediation, and why?$$,$$Create a new restore point right away, so that any future rollback lands on a known-clean, post-remediation state rather than an old point that predates the cleanup.$$),
('a2-f-931','aplus2','core',3,'3.3',$$Why is "educate the user" the final step rather than something skipped once the system is clean?$$,$$Technical cleanup fixes the current infection, but if the user doesn't understand how it happened, such as clicking a phishing link or disabling security warnings, they're likely to repeat the behavior and get reinfected - education addresses the root cause.$$),
('a2-f-932','aplus2','core',3,'3.3',$$A technician quarantines a system, disables System Restore, then remediates. What is the correct next step before educating the user?$$,$$Schedule ongoing scans and run available updates, then re-enable System Restore and create a fresh restore point - only after that is the user education step performed.$$),
('a2-f-933','aplus2','core',3,'3.3',$$What risk does skipping the "disable System Restore" step create, even if remediation itself is thorough?$$,$$A restore point created before or during the infection may already contain the malware; without disabling System Restore first, a later restore operation could silently reintroduce the infection even though the current files were cleaned.$$),
('a2-f-934','aplus2','core',3,'3.3',$$How do you confirm remediation succeeded before moving on to scheduling scans/updates?$$,$$Run a follow-up scan and confirm it comes back clean, and verify the original symptoms, such as pop-ups, slow performance, or hijacked settings, are gone - moving forward on an unconfirmed cleanup risks scheduling protection for a system that's still infected.$$),
('a2-f-935','aplus2','core',3,'3.3',$$Besides removing the malware itself, what should remediation also consider regarding how the infection got in?$$,$$Identify and close the entry vector if possible, such as an unpatched vulnerability, a malicious email attachment, or a risky browser extension, so the same route can't be used again before the ongoing-protection steps take effect.$$),
('a2-f-936','aplus2','acronym',3,'3.3',$$AUP$$,$$Acceptable Use Policy - an organizational document outlining permitted behavior on company systems; educating the user after a malware incident often includes reinforcing relevant AUP points, such as not installing unauthorized software or disabling security tools.$$),
('a2-f-937','aplus2','acronym',3,'3.3',$$IOC$$,$$Indicator of Compromise - an artifact, such as an unusual file, registry key, network connection, or process name, observed during investigation/verification that helps confirm a system is actually infected before quarantine and remediation begin.$$),
('a2-f-938','aplus2','feynman',3,'3.3',$$Explain why the malware removal process treats "schedule scans and run updates" as a separate step from "remediate," rather than folding it into remediation.$$,$$Model answer: Remediation is reactive - it deals with the infection that's already there, right now, on this machine. Scheduling scans and updates is proactive - it's about preventing the next infection and catching anything that slipped past the current cleanup. Folding them together would blur an important distinction: remediation has a clear endpoint, the scan comes back clean, while ongoing scanning and patching is a continuous, standing configuration the technician sets up so the system stays protected long after the visit ends. Separating them as steps also enforces sequencing - you shouldn't set up that ongoing protection before you've confirmed today's infection is actually gone.$$),
-- ==== 3.4 Mobile OS/app issues (16) ====
('a2-f-939','aplus2','core',3,'3.4',$$An app crashes immediately after a mobile OS update. What's the first thing to check?$$,$$Check whether the app developer has released an update or documented compatibility fix for the new OS version - post-update crashes are commonly caused by the app not yet supporting a changed OS component.$$),
('a2-f-940','aplus2','core',3,'3.4',$$What's the correct escalation order for troubleshooting a mobile app that repeatedly crashes?$$,$$From least to most disruptive: restart the app/phone, clear the app's cache, clear the app's data or reinstall it, and only factory reset the device if nothing less drastic resolves the issue.$$),
('a2-f-941','aplus2','core',3,'3.4',$$A phone's battery suddenly drains much faster than before with no change in usage. What's the diagnostic first step?$$,$$Check the device's battery usage statistics to identify which specific app or process is consuming excess power, rather than assuming a hardware battery failure.$$),
('a2-f-942','aplus2','core',3,'3.4',$$A phone feels hot even when the user isn't actively using it. What does this suggest?$$,$$Unexplained heat during apparent idle time usually means an app or process is actively running and consuming CPU in the background - check running processes/battery usage to identify it.$$),
('a2-f-943','aplus2','core',3,'3.4',$$A phone drops Wi-Fi in a spot where other devices connect fine. Where's the fault most likely, and what's a common fix?$$,$$The fault is most likely with that phone's saved network profile for that specific Wi-Fi network - forgetting the network and reconnecting, re-entering credentials, commonly resolves it.$$),
('a2-f-944','aplus2','core',3,'3.4',$$A phone has full cellular signal bars but data is extremely slow or keeps dropping. What setting should be checked?$$,$$APN (Access Point Name) settings, or whether a carrier settings update is available - a data-path misconfiguration can cause poor data performance even with strong signal.$$),
('a2-f-945','aplus2','core',3,'3.4',$$What does it mean if an app has camera or microphone permission the user doesn't recall granting?$$,$$It's a potential privacy/security concern - unauthorized or unnecessary sensitive permissions should be reviewed and revoked in the device's app permissions settings.$$),
('a2-f-946','aplus2','core',3,'3.4',$$An app meant to sync small text data is shown consuming several gigabytes of mobile data. What does this suggest?$$,$$This suggests the app may be malicious, misconfigured, or misbehaving, consuming data far beyond its stated purpose, and warrants investigation, permission review, or removal.$$),
('a2-f-947','aplus2','core',3,'3.4',$$What two general causes typically explain gradual, unexplained mobile device slowdown over time?$$,$$Background apps accumulating and consuming resources, and low available storage space - both are commonly checked and addressed before considering more drastic steps like a factory reset.$$),
('a2-f-948','aplus2','core',3,'3.4',$$What combination of symptoms should make a technician suspect unauthorized account access on a mobile device, rather than a normal performance issue?$$,$$Login notifications or account activity from an unrecognized device/location, and/or unfamiliar apps appearing on the device that the user never installed - these point at compromise rather than routine performance variation.$$),
('a2-f-949','aplus2','core',3,'3.4',$$Why might sync settings, not malware, explain high mobile data usage from a legitimate app?$$,$$An app configured to sync full-resolution photos or videos over cellular, instead of waiting for Wi-Fi, can legitimately consume large amounts of data - checking the app's sync/data-usage settings should happen before assuming malware.$$),
('a2-f-950','aplus2','core',3,'3.4',$$What's the difference between clearing an app's cache versus clearing its data when troubleshooting crashes?$$,$$Clearing cache removes temporary files without affecting saved settings or login state, a lighter first step; clearing data resets the app to its just-installed state, losing local settings/logins, a deeper reset tried when clearing cache alone doesn't fix repeated crashes.$$),
('a2-f-951','aplus2','core',3,'3.4',$$A user's mobile device connects to Wi-Fi fine but cellular data performance is poor in an area with strong signal. Name two configuration items worth checking.$$,$$The APN (Access Point Name) settings, and whether an available carrier settings update has been installed - both directly affect the cellular data path independent of signal strength.$$),
('a2-f-952','aplus2','acronym',3,'3.4',$$APN$$,$$Access Point Name - the carrier-defined settings a mobile device uses to establish a connection to the cellular data network; incorrect or missing APN settings can cause data connectivity or performance problems even with full signal.$$),
('a2-f-953','aplus2','acronym',3,'3.4',$$OS$$,$$Operating System - the platform, such as Android or iOS, an app runs on; app crashes right after an OS update are commonly due to the app not yet being updated for compatibility with OS changes.$$),
('a2-f-954','aplus2','feynman',3,'3.4',$$Explain why "no change in usage habits" matters when a user reports sudden mobile battery drain.$$,$$Model answer: Battery capacity and app behavior both degrade or fluctuate slowly under normal conditions, so a sudden drop with the user's habits unchanged rules out the simplest explanation, that the user is just doing more, and points instead to something new happening on the device: a misbehaving app update, a stuck background process, malware, or a hardware fault in the battery itself. Ruling out a behavior change first is what justifies moving straight to checking battery usage statistics rather than dismissing the complaint as normal wear.$$),
-- ==== 3.5 Mobile OS security issues (18) ====
('a2-f-955','aplus2','core',3,'3.5',$$A phone's location indicator is active even though no navigation app is in use. What does this suggest and what should be checked?$$,$$This suggests an app is tracking location in the background without obvious need - check the device's app permissions for location access, particularly any app granted "always allow"/background access, and revoke what isn't necessary.$$),
('a2-f-956','aplus2','core',3,'3.5',$$What does sustained high background network traffic on an otherwise idle phone suggest?$$,$$It's a common sign of spyware or malware communicating with a remote server, sending collected data out or receiving commands - investigate which app or process is generating the traffic.$$),
('a2-f-957','aplus2','core',3,'3.5',$$A user's private photos appear online without their consent. What are the likely causes to investigate?$$,$$Likely causes include a compromised cloud backup account linked to the phone, a malicious or overly permissioned app with storage access, or unauthorized physical/remote access to the device - all should be checked.$$),
('a2-f-958','aplus2','core',3,'3.5',$$What's the first action when a cloud account tied to a mobile device shows sign-in activity from an unrecognized device or location?$$,$$Immediately change the account password and enable, or verify, multi-factor authentication - this cuts off the unauthorized access path before further investigation.$$),
('a2-f-959','aplus2','core',3,'3.5',$$Why is unauthorized rooting/jailbreaking treated as a serious mobile security issue?$$,$$Root/jailbreak access removes the OS's built-in sandboxing and security boundaries device-wide, which can let malware escalate privileges, bypass MDM policy enforcement, and access data or system functions normally off-limits to any single app.$$),
('a2-f-960','aplus2','core',3,'3.5',$$What should be reviewed on an app suspected of accessing more personal data, such as contacts or messages, than it needs?$$,$$Its granted permissions, such as contacts, SMS, and storage, compared against what the app's stated function actually requires - excessive permissions relative to purpose are the mechanism such apps use to access data they shouldn't.$$),
('a2-f-961','aplus2','core',3,'3.5',$$How can a technician tell the difference between normal app location use and unauthorized background tracking?$$,$$Check whether location access is limited to "while using the app" versus granted as "always allow"; a persistent location indicator with no relevant app actively open points to unwanted background tracking via an always-allow permission.$$),
('a2-f-962','aplus2','core',3,'3.5',$$On a corporate-managed device, why does an unauthorized root/jailbreak often trigger an immediate incident response rather than a routine fix?$$,$$Because root access can disable or bypass the MDM agent that enforces corporate security policy on the device, meaning the organization can no longer trust or control the device until it's re-imaged or restored.$$),
('a2-f-963','aplus2','core',3,'3.5',$$What combination of symptoms points to spyware performing both tracking and data exfiltration on a phone?$$,$$A location indicator active without a legitimate app in use, together with unexplained high background data usage - each alone could have another cause, but together they strongly suggest an app is both tracking and sending data out.$$),
('a2-f-964','aplus2','core',3,'3.5',$$What are the recommended steps, in order, after a user reports their cloud-linked account was accessed from an unrecognized device?$$,$$Verify the alert itself isn't a phishing attempt, change the password immediately, enable/verify MFA, review recent account activity for other signs of compromise, and sign out of all other active sessions.$$),
('a2-f-965','aplus2','core',3,'3.5',$$Why does an unauthorized jailbreak on iOS increase malware risk even if the user hasn't installed anything unusual yet?$$,$$Jailbreaking removes Apple's App Store vetting and sandboxing protections system-wide, so the device becomes capable of installing and running unofficial software with elevated access - the exposure exists the moment the jailbreak happens, independent of what gets installed afterward.$$),
('a2-f-966','aplus2','core',3,'3.5',$$A phone shows both unusually high data usage and a device the user doesn't recognize listed in their cloud account's active sessions. What should be prioritized?$$,$$Prioritize securing the account first - change the password, enable MFA, and terminate the unrecognized session - since a compromised account can itself be driving or masking the unusual local activity.$$),
('a2-f-967','aplus2','core',3,'3.5',$$What's a practical way to audit which apps have background location access on a phone?$$,$$Open the OS's privacy/permissions settings and filter by location - it lists every app with access and shows whether each is set to "while using," "always," or "ask every time," making it straightforward to revoke unnecessary "always" grants.$$),
('a2-f-968','aplus2','core',3,'3.5',$$Why should leaked personal files be investigated at both the account level and the device/app level?$$,$$The leak could originate from a compromised cloud account on the server side, from a malicious or over-permissioned local app with storage access on the device side, or both - securing only one layer might leave the actual source of the leak unaddressed.$$),
('a2-f-969','aplus2','acronym',3,'3.5',$$MDM$$,$$Mobile Device Management - software that lets organizations enforce security policy, configuration, and compliance on managed mobile devices; unauthorized rooting/jailbreaking can disable or bypass MDM enforcement, which is why it's treated as a critical incident on corporate devices.$$),
('a2-f-970','aplus2','acronym',3,'3.5',$$MFA$$,$$Multi-Factor Authentication - requiring a second verification factor beyond the password; enabling MFA is a top-priority response after unauthorized account access is discovered, since it blocks reuse of a stolen password alone.$$),
('a2-f-971','aplus2','acronym',3,'3.5',$$GPS$$,$$Global Positioning System - the satellite-based location service mobile devices use; unexplained, persistent location indicator activity with no relevant app in active use is a key symptom of unauthorized location tracking.$$),
('a2-f-972','aplus2','feynman',3,'3.5',$$Explain why unauthorized root or jailbreak access is considered more dangerous than simply installing a suspicious app.$$,$$Model answer: A suspicious app, even a malicious one, normally still operates inside the OS's sandbox - it's limited to the permissions the user granted it, and other apps and system functions stay walled off. Rooting or jailbreaking removes that wall entirely: it grants system-level privileges that let software bypass the sandbox, read or modify other apps' data, disable built-in security features, including the corporate MDM agent that's supposed to enforce policy, and persist in ways ordinary apps can't. So a single bad app is a contained problem with a known blast radius; unauthorized root/jailbreak access turns the whole device into an open surface, which is why it gets treated as a device-wide compromise rather than an app-specific one.$$);
